    "learningObjective": "Understand the historical genesis of the MTP Act 1971 under the Shantilal Shah Committee, its relationship with BNS Sections 90-92 (IPC 312-314), and constitutional bodily autonomy under Article 21.",
    "introduction": "The Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act, 1971 (Act No. 34 of 1971) was enacted on 10th August 1971 and came into force on 1st April 1972. It carved out a comprehensive statutory exception to the criminal offence of causing miscarriage under the Indian Penal Code, transforming abortion from a criminal crime into a healthcare entitlement regulated by law.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Legislative Genesis: Shantilal Shah Committee (1966)</h3>\n<p>Prior to 1971, causing miscarriage in India was criminalized under Section 312 IPC, punishable with up to 7 years imprisonment unless done in good faith for saving the woman's life. This led to dangerous underground clandestine abortions causing thousands of maternal deaths. In 1964, the Government of India appointed the <strong>Shantilal Shah Committee</strong> to examine abortion laws. The Committee submitted its landmark report in 1966, recommending legalization of abortion on broad health, humanitarian, and socio-economic grounds, leading directly to Act No. 34 of 1971.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Interplay with BNS / IPC Criminal Provisions</h3>\n<p>Under Section 90 of Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (Section 312 IPC), causing miscarriage voluntarily remains an offence. However, Section 3 of the MTP Act operates with a strong <strong>non-obstante clause</strong>:</p>\n<p><em>'Notwithstanding anything contained in the Indian Penal Code (BNS), a registered medical practitioner shall not be guilty of any offence under that Code or under any other law... if pregnancy is terminated in accordance with the provisions of this Act.'</em></p>\n\n<h3>3. Constitutional Bodily Autonomy under Article 21</h3>\n<p>In <em>K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017)</em> and <em>X v. Principal Secretary, Health and Family Welfare Dept., Delhi (2022)</em>, the Supreme Court held that reproductive choices and bodily integrity are fundamental aspects of personal liberty and privacy under Article 21. A woman has the constitutional right to decide whether or not to carry a pregnancy to term, subject only to statutory medical safeguards.</p>\n",
