    "subtitle": "Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2021 (Act No. 8 of 2021) Features",
    "learningObjective": "Master the progressive shifts introduced by the MTP (Amendment) Act, 2021, especially replacing 'married woman and her husband' with 'woman and her partner' and elevating gestational limits.",
    "introduction": "The Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2021 (Act No. 8 of 2021) came into force on 24th September 2021. It modernized India's abortion jurisprudence by expanding gestational ceilings from 20 to 24 weeks and replacing archaic marital references with inclusive gender-neutral partnerships.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Substitution of 'Woman and her Partner'</h3>\n<p>Under the original 1971 Act, Explanation II to Section 3(2) permitted termination where pregnancy resulted from failure of a contraceptive device used by <em>'any married woman or her husband'</em>. This excluded unmarried women from claiming contraceptive failure as a ground for abortion.</p>\n<p><strong>The 2021 Reform:</strong> The 2021 Amendment substituted the words <strong>'any woman or her partner'</strong> in place of 'any married woman or her husband'. Consequently, contraceptive failure is now an equal statutory ground for unmarried, single, divorced, or widowed women.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Expansion of Gestational Ceilings</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>First Tier:</strong> Up to <strong>20 weeks</strong> (increased from 12 weeks for single doctor opinion). Only <strong>ONE (1) RMP</strong> opinion is now needed up to 20 weeks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Tier:</strong> <strong>20 to 24 weeks</strong> on the opinion of <strong>TWO (2) RMPs</strong> for prescribed vulnerable categories of women.</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Tier:</strong> <strong>Beyond 24 weeks</strong> without any gestational limit for substantial foetal abnormalities approved by a State Medical Board.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Protection of Privacy: Section 5A</h3>\n<p>The 2021 Amendment inserted <strong>Section 5A</strong>, making it a criminal offence for any medical practitioner to reveal the name and identity of a woman whose pregnancy has been terminated, punishable with imprisonment up to 1 year.</p>\n",
