    "subjectId": "soc-pocso",
    "title": "Statutory Presumptions (S. 29 Presumption of Guilt, S. 30 Culpable Mental State) & S. 42A Overriding Effect",
    "subtitle": "Sections 29, 30 Reverse Burdens, Foundational Facts Doctrine & Section 42A Non-Obstante Priority",
    "learningObjective": "Master the operation of statutory reverse burdens of proof under Sections 29 and 30, and the overriding supremacy of the POCSO Act under Section 42A.",
    "introduction": "Sections 29 and 30 contain the most potent evidentiary provisions of the POCSO Act. Deviating from the classical criminal presumption of innocence, the statute mandates that the Special Court shall presume that the accused committed the offence and possessed a culpable mental state, once foundational facts are established.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Statutory Presumption as to Certain Offences (Section 29)</h3>\n<p>Section 29 creates a statutory presumption of guilt:</p>\n<p><em>'Where a person is prosecuted for committing or abetting or attempting to commit any offence under <strong>Section 3, 5, 7 or Section 9</strong> of this Act, the Special Court <strong>SHALL PRESUME that such person has committed or abetted or attempted to commit the offence</strong>, as the case may be, unless the contrary is proved.'</em></p>\n\n<h3>2. The Foundational Facts Requirement for Section 29</h3>\n<p>In <em>Dharmander Singh v. State (Govt of NCT of Delhi), (2020)</em> and <em>State of UP v. Sonu (2021)</em>, the courts settled how Section 29 operates:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Section 29 does NOT apply in a vacuum merely upon framing of charges.</li>\n  <li>The prosecution must first prove the <strong>foundational facts</strong> (that the victim is a child below 18 years, and that sexual assault or penetration occurred).</li>\n  <li>Once these primary foundational facts are established, the legal burden shifts to the accused to prove his innocence on a balance of probabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Presumption of Culpable Mental State (Section 30)</h3>\n<p>In any prosecution for any offence under this Act which requires a <strong>culpable mental state</strong> on the part of the accused, the Special Court <strong>SHALL PRESUME the existence of such mental state</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Under Section 30(2), 'culpable mental state' includes intention, motive, knowledge of a fact and belief in, or reason to believe, a fact.</li>\n  <li>The accused must prove that he had no such mental state beyond reasonable doubt or on high probability.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Overriding Effect of the Act (Section 42A)</h3>\n<p>Inserted by the 2019 Amendment, Section 42A establishes statutory supremacy:</p>\n<p><em>'The provisions of this Act shall have effect <strong>notwithstanding anything inconsistent contained in any other law</strong> for the time being in force, or any custom or usage, or any instrument having effect by virtue of any law.'</em></p>\n<p><em>Application:</em> If there is any conflict between POCSO and the Indian Penal Code (BNS), CrPC (BNSS), Indian Evidence Act (BSA), or personal laws, the <strong>POCSO Act PREVAILS absolutely</strong>.</p>\n",
